@startuml request-flow
' ============================================================================
'  pc-agent - Runtime flow of ONE user message through all layers
'  (the dynamic view: time flows downward; the reason->act LOOP is the key part)
'  Render (from this folder):  java -jar ../../../plantuml.jar -tpng request-flow.puml
' ============================================================================

skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam ParticipantPadding 14
skinparam BoxPadding 12
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFF8DC
  BorderColor #C9B458
}

title pc-agent - Runtime Flow of One Message (time flows down; the loop is the heart)

box "USER'S MACHINE  (local - plaintext + key live here)" #E6F4EA
  actor User
  participant "UI\n(web / desktop)" as UI
  participant "Gateway\n(localhost WS,\ntoken-gated)" as GW
  participant "Agent core\n(loop / engine)" as CORE
  participant "Memory\n(local cache\n+ E2E vault)" as MEM
  participant "Policy +\nApproval" as POL
  participant "Tools\n(files/exec/browser)" as TOOL
  participant "Skills\n(SKILL.md\nplaybooks)" as SKILL
end box

box "REMOTE  (your cloud / their key)" #FDECEA
  participant "LLM endpoint\n(secure VM / BYOK / local)" as LLM
  participant "Control plane\n(auth, usage, vault sync)" as CP
end box

note over User, CP
  Precondition: user already registered/logged in. The Gateway validated the
  per-launch local token; the user's entitlements (tier/quota) are available to Policy.
end note

User -> UI : type a message
UI -> GW : chat.send {session, text}\n(127.0.0.1 WS + token)
GW -> CORE : handle_message(session, text)

== Load context (all local) ==
CORE -> MEM : load_history(session)
MEM --> CORE : prior messages (decrypted locally)
CORE -> MEM : recall(query)  // relevant long-term memory
MEM --> CORE : memories
CORE -> MEM : append(user message)   // persist
note over CORE : build the request = system prompt + history\n+ recalled memory + tool specs + SKILL one-liners (descriptions only)

== Reason -> act LOOP ==
loop until the LLM returns a final answer (no tool calls)
  CORE -> LLM : stream(system_prompt, messages, tools)\n--- HTTPS, plaintext to YOUR secure model ---
  LLM --> CORE : stream: thinking + text deltas + tool calls
  CORE -> UI : forward deltas (live) via Gateway

  opt request matches an advertised skill (e.g. browser-automation)
    CORE -> SKILL : read(SKILL.md path)   // an ordinary read tool call
    SKILL --> CORE : full playbook -> enters messages[]
    note over CORE, SKILL : the agent now follows that skill's step-by-step\nguidance for the rest of the run (read once, stays in history)
  end

  alt LLM asked for tool call(s)
    CORE -> POL : authorize(tool, args, entitlements)
    alt sensitive / irreversible (e.g. send email, destructive exec)
      POL -> UI : approval request
      UI --> POL : user says Yes / No
    end
    POL --> CORE : allow / deny
    CORE -> TOOL : execute(tool, args)   // runs on THIS PC
    TOOL --> CORE : result
    CORE -> MEM : append(tool result)    // persist
    note over CORE, LLM : results are appended to messages ->\nthe loop sends them BACK to the LLM for the next step
  else no tool calls -> this text is the final answer
    note over CORE : exit the loop
  end
end

== Finish ==
CORE -> MEM : append(final answer) -> encrypt
MEM -> CP : sync CIPHERTEXT (async)
CORE -> CP : report usage (async, metadata only)
CORE -> UI : final answer (already streamed)
UI -> User : show answer

note over LLM
  The LLM is STATELESS: each call gets the full
  messages[]; it remembers nothing between calls.
  "Memory" is the agent feeding history back in,
  not the model retaining anything.
end note

note over CORE, SKILL
  SKILLS vs TOOLS: tools are callable ACTIONS; skills are
  loadable KNOW-HOW (markdown playbooks). Only each skill's
  one-line description is in the prompt; the body is pulled
  in on demand via the read tool when the task matches —
  so you can add many skills with no prompt bloat and no code.
end note

@enduml
